    "result_text": "Build is green \u2014 `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` and `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nReplaced the direct level rules with a points-then-level system across three files:\n\n**`MembershipTier.java`** \u2014 rewrote as two small, cohesive static methods:\n- `points(...)`: starts at 0, `+2` email present, `+1` namesakeCount == 0, `+2` for a household of 3+ (`namesakeCount >= 2`, since the household is the new owner plus `namesakeCount` existing members \u2014 a single-member household is `namesakeCount == 0`), `+3` tenure over 365 days.\n- `level(points)`: maps to 1 (0\u20131), 2 (2\u20133), 3 (4\u20135), 4 (6+).\n\n**`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added the `membershipPoints` mapping and changed `membershipLevel` to derive from the points, so the DTO returns both fields.\n\n**`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `membershipPoints` schema property and updated `membershipLevel` (now max 4) with the new description; this regenerates `OwnerDto` with the new getter/setter.\n\nThe verifying test (`coreReturnsPointsAndLevel`) confirms a fresh owner with an email and no namesake scores `membershipPoints=3` \u2192 `membershipLevel=2`.\n\nNote on the \"household of 3 or more\" factor: I read household size as `namesakeCount + 1` (the test comment calls `namesakeCount == 0` a \"single-member household\"), so the +2 triggers at `namesakeCount >= 2`. The one test in the suite doesn't exercise that branch, so if your intended reading is `namesakeCount >= 3` instead, that's a one-line change.\n\n**Structural impact:** kept every touched function simple (each new method has CC \u2264 4 and sits in a tiny class with near-zero `WMC_other`), added the household rule as a new small unit rather than growing an existing method, and touched the minimum three files needed.",
